module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unrun_self_witnessed_module_reads_as_coverage

import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }
import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }

data unrun_self_witnessed_module_reads_as_coverage: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "unrun_self_witnessed_module_reads_as_coverage" as NonEmptyStr,
  receipts: [
    "CLASS: a module in a `witness_layer_roots` layer declares `test fn` bodies whose only audience is its own file, and the file sits outside `gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` `witness_discovery_scan_dirs`. CI never discovers it, so the assertions never execute -- yet the test syntax reads as coverage to every reader after it, including the reader who wrote it. This is rung honesty (DESIGN section 4b) applied to a whole file rather than one name: a type name, a diagnostic variant, an inert lens or a plan establishes nothing, and neither does an unrun `test fn`.",
    "INVALID STATE: a file passes the compiler, contains test bodies that would fail if the code they describe regressed, and contributes zero refusal to CI. The mutation the tests exist to catch can be introduced and the floor stays green. The state is invisible precisely because the file looks like a witness: `test fn` is a rung marker, and nothing distinguishes a discovered one from an undiscovered one at the site where it is read.",
    "FOUND BY: reading the source, which is no rung at all -- and that is the finding. DESIGN section 4b defines four rungs (construction over proof, proof over validation, validation over mitigation) and says \"Silent wrongness is not a rung: it is below the ladder and forbidden outright\". An unrun `test fn` whose guarded mutation stays green IS that silent wrongness, so this class lives below the ladder rather than at its foot, and nothing in the toolchain placed it anywhere: it was noticed by a human reading `src/v2/workflow/orchestration_while_emit.dag` at 0dc3f0ab4c1fe112c13b10d6cbc991a94f3744fd while deciding whether a deletion was safe. There it carried the only other assertion in the tree pinning one bracket operator per `std.filesystem.types` `EntryKind`; deleting the production route through that lowering turned NOTHING red anywhere -- compiles stayed at `0 blocking error(s)` and no witness count moved. A defect that survives every automated check available is what \"below the ladder\" means, which is why it is recorded here instead of fixed by a check nobody has yet been able to write.",
    "POPULATION AS A DATED READING, NOT AS AUTHORITY -- every figure in this row was taken on 2026-10-19 at origin/main 45fdd50224891a6414e00ddd01132493e1113233, and a reading at a named commit is not a live claim (DESIGN section 6: a measurement not worth an entry point is a one-off, not an instrument). THIS ROW HAS NO ENTRY POINT YET, and it says so rather than dressing one up: there is no corpus-enumeration producer in the language -- `v2.lens.mandatory_tag.corpus_scan` carries the modules it scans as written data -- so binding a `gunbc test <label>` today would be an instrument reporting coverage it never performs, which is this row's own subject committed inside the row about it. A hand-written shell script is also refused here: Charter 2's exit is `tools/*.sh` = 0, and DESIGN section 6 lists a hand-authored script implementing semantics expressible in `.dag` as a tell. THE FOLLOW-UP THAT SUPERSEDES EVERY NUMBER BELOW is therefore a `.dag` producer that reads `witness_layer_roots` and `witness_discovery_scan_dirs` from `gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` and is bound as a `gunbc test <label>` row in `gunbc.instrument_targets`; until it lands, treat these as readings a reader can re-derive by hand, not as a check anyone runs. HOW TO RE-DERIVE: count `test fn` bodies in every `.dag` under the two `witness_layer_roots` (`dag`, `src/v2`), excluding paths under any `witness_discovery_scan_dirs` entry and excluding the `test` family -- both lists read from the authority at the cited commit, not copied here, and they are redundant with each other at that commit because every enrolled scan dir also sits under a `test` path. RESULT AT THAT COMMIT: 7 modules / 67 bodies, `--include-test-family` gives 888 / 6320, `--enrol src/v2/workflow` gives 2 / 10. It is a shell tool and not a `gunbc test <label>` because a label needs a producer that runs it and no producer exists for corpus enumeration -- `v2.lens.mandatory_tag.corpus_scan` carries the modules it scans as written data -- so a label today would be an instrument reporting coverage it never performs, which is this row's own subject; when a corpus-enumeration producer lands, the label supersedes the script. OBSERVED 2026-10-19 at origin/main 45fdd50224891a6414e00ddd01132493e1113233:: 7 modules / 67 bodies -- orchestration_retry_emit 17, orchestration_emit 16, orchestration_bash 12, orchestration_tier2_emit 8, lens/mandatory_tag/corpus_scan_witness 8, orchestration_while_emit 4, lens/no_dual_representation 2. The second `case` is load-bearing; dropping it is the trap named in the next receipt. Per-function enrolment was measured too, because a module can be unrostered while single functions are named elsewhere: for each name above, `grep -rl <name> --include=*.dag .` minus its own file, keeping only hits where the name sits in a registry position (`function:` / `fn_name:` / `functions:`) -> 4 of 67, all in `src/v2/lens/mandatory_tag/corpus_scan_witness.dag` (`corpus_live_clean_tree_wall_holds`, `corpus_projection_missing_plant_reads_absent`, `corpus_projection_file_plant_reads_non_external_scheme`, `corpus_projection_real_anchor_reads_https`), named as `ClaimRun` entries in `gunbc.instruments.extdeps_external_authority_transport`. Those do not execute: the only discovered consumer of that list, `test.claim.cheap_gate_pool`, asserts the pool's CHUNKING (`claims_covered_by_cheap_pool_chunks`) and never runs the entries; and `gunbc.declined_live_tree_defect_classification`, which names two of them, says of itself it \"is not an expected-red enrolment and must never become one\". Net claim: 0 of 67 executed by any path in CI. Stated honestly, the loose version of that query -- any mention, no registry-position filter -- gives 48 of 67, so the inflated figure is available and is not the claim; the claim is about execution, and its decidable form is that registry-position count plus the two consumers named above.",
    "THE COUNTING DISCIPLINE THIS ROW OWES, because the naive figure is wrong and was nearly used. Dropping that `test`-family exclusion returns 888 files / 6320 bodies at the same head (verified: dropping the scan-dir exclusion alone still gives 7/67 today, because every enrolled scan dir also sits under a `*/test/*` path -- the two filters are redundant now and diverge the moment one is enrolled outside `test/`, which is exactly what the TRIGGER receipt contemplates; both are therefore kept). That 888/6320 measures the gap between one discovery mechanism and the whole corpus -- it counts `src/v2/test/**`, whose entries are run BY NAME from explicit registries -- and publishing it as the unrun population would be this row's own failure mode committed inside the row about it. So the population is the filtered 7/67 and the filter is named. Second discipline: a cost row is NOT an execution receipt. 43 of the 67 have `eval_ms_basis` rows in `gunbc.witness.witness_row_cost_basis.tsv`, which reads like proof someone ran them. Measured against the same population at the cited commit: 43 of 67, and the table behind it -- 1146 data rows, ONE distinct `run_ref` (30550328673), one host_class (`srv_fleet_arm64`): a single seeding batch for the entire corpus, and the file's own header says a row is established by \"first fleet observation\". It evidences one historical out-of-CI run and nothing about today. Hence this row claims the decidable thing -- no path in CI discovers or runs them -- and refuses the unfalsifiable one (never executes, anywhere, ever).",
    "HARM: the assertions can regress silently and every downstream reader is misled twice -- once by the file's own `test fn` markers, and again by any prose that cites the module as the reason a behavior is covered. The `While` emit arm (`compiler.emit_orchestration` `orch_emit_while_step`) is the live example: its only falsifier was an undiscovered self-test, so a malformed While lowering ships green, while `docs/plans/shell-to-dag-residual-census-and-arc-completion.md` and `gunbc.plans.shell_emission_model` each described While emit as covered.",
    "WHAT THE PORTED COVERAGE IS AND IS NOT, stated so nobody reads it as production safety: the 9 tests PROPOSED in pull request #14489 as `test.claim.path_predicate_bash_lowering_witness` (including the 3 that pin the `$(dirname \"$name\")` ascent and its two POSIX-name guards) -- pending, not merged, and absent from `main` at this head, so nothing here should be read as existing coverage until that pull request lands -- exercise a TYPED SUBSTRATE WITH NO PRODUCTION CALLER. Nothing emitted today passes through `bash_path_predicate_target_model` or `bash_dirname_ascent_target_model`: the production line that used them was deleted as a no-consumer chain, and the only remaining callers are `v2.workflow.orchestration_while_emit`, which CI never discovers, and that witness itself. They are therefore coverage of a substrate kept for a hypothetical future typed path-test route, plus a guard against a future emitter shipping `d;rm -rf /` into an emitted program if that route is ever taken -- they are NOT evidence that any emitted program is safe today, and they must not be cited as if they were. Whether to keep the substrate at all is the operator's enrolment decision named in the TRIGGER receipt, not a fact this row can settle.",
    "CEILING: STRUCTURAL, NOT DECIDABLE FROM THE NODE TREE, AND THIS IS THE DEFICIT. `test fn` carries no discovered-ness fact, and discovery is a directory predicate read at run time by `src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs` (`witness_discovery_scan_dirs()`), so a module in a `witness_layer_roots` layer compiles identically whether or not anyone will ever execute its tests. The compiler cannot refuse it because the fact is not in the declaration. A rung-3 alternative exists and was NOT taken here because it is a CI-policy change, wider than a ledger row: a coherence witness that refuses when a module in a layer root declares `test fn` bodies, lies outside every scan dir, and is named by no execution roster -- turning the population into a refusal instead of a fact. `gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` already registers namespaces that are deliberately non-executing (`non_executing_witness_module_prefixes` = [\"v1\"]), so the distinction between 'deliberately unrun' and 'unrun by accident' is expressible in the authority and simply is not enforced for the layer roots.",
    "TRIGGER -- what dissolves this row: an operator CI-policy decision. Either (a) add `src/v2/workflow` (and the two `src/v2/lens` files) to `witness_discovery_scan_dirs`, which makes the 67 assertions execute but also pulls in the whole self-witnessed family and its cost, or (b) delete the population along with the code it guards, or (c) install the coherence witness above so an unrun self-witness refuses at compile time. The decision is outward-facing (it changes what CI executes and pays for) and belongs to the operator, not to whoever notices. Until then the population has no entry point at all: it is a dated reading at the commit named in the POPULATION receipt, re-derivable by hand from the two authority lists, and the missing `.dag` producer plus its `gunbc test` label is the follow-up that turns it into an instrument. MEASUREMENT NOTE, so this row does not itself become stale prose: the population is a function of two lists in `gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` plus the tree, so it is re-derived, never copied. If scan dirs widen, the population shrinks automatically and the receipts above are dated observations about the two heads they name (origin/main 45fdd5022489 for the population, 0dc3f0ab4c1f for the discovery) -- which is exactly how a dated capture should read.",
    "ISLAND NAMED AS THE DELETION TRIGGER'S POPULATION -- PROPOSED, NOT BUILT, AND HEADED AS MEASURED. Scope, in the order the consumers fall away: `src/v2/workflow/orchestration_while_emit.dag` has 0 importers, 4 unrun self-tests, and is the only constructor of `PipelineStep::While` outside `src/v2/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize.dag` -- true at origin/main 45fdd5022489 and unchanged by any PR in flight. Behind it sits the spelling chain `test.extdeps.languages.bash_orch_if` `bash_dirname_ascent_target_model` -> `v2.extdeps.languages.bash_orch_tier2` `orch_cmdsubst_assign_call_target_model` -> `orch_call_target_model` -> `bash_orch_call_target_model`. MEASURED HEADS DISAGREE ABOUT WHO ELSE HOLDS THE DOOR, so each is stated: at origin/main 45fdd5022489 `bash_dirname_ascent_target_model` has TWO callers, `v2.workflow.orchestration_while_emit` `orch_while_body_model_text` and `test.extdeps.languages.bash_orch_if` `bash_nearest_ancestor_locate_body_command`; at the head of the carve PR #14489 the second is deleted, leaving `v2.workflow.orchestration_while_emit` `orch_while_body_model_text` as the only caller outside the definition, and only then is the chain reachable from nowhere but an undiscovered self-test. smart-heron-455's ladder cut removes the `realize_call:` binding in `v2.extdeps.languages.bash_orchestration_emit`, which is the other way in. The `PathPredicate` lowering `bash_path_predicate_target_model` is entered externally only by `orchestration_while_emit` plus the witness proposed in pull request #14489, which is PENDING -- if that pull request is closed or renamed, this receipt's `test.claim.path_predicate_bash_lowering_witness` citations go stale with it and the lowering has no external entrant at all. Deleting the whole unit also strands `std.path_intent` `DirnameAscent`, `PathPredicate`/`PathEntryKind`/`PathNotEq`, and `extdeps.linux.proc_self_cgroup` `proc_self_cgroup_absolute_path_from_relative` -- the last has a consumer at main (`src/v2/extdeps/languages/bash_proc_self_cgroup.dag` `bash_membership_assign_from_record`) and so is LISTED, not deleted, until #14467 removes that file. RECOMMENDATION: do NOT delete in one PR while the trigger above is undecided. If scan-dir enrolment is chosen, those 67 assertions become the cheapest real coverage in the tree and the island is worth keeping; deleting first and enrolling second throws away tests that would have worked. The query in the POPULATION receipt, not this paragraph, is the authority on what is in the population -- re-run it at the head you stand on, because the two PRs in flight move which callers exist.",
  ],
  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots", decl_name: "witness_discovery_scan_dirs"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots", decl_name: "witness_layer_roots"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots", decl_name: "non_executing_witness_module_prefixes"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.workflow.orchestration_while_emit", decl_name: "orch_while_path_kind_discriminates_regular_directory_missing_holds"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "v2.workflow.orchestration_retry_emit", decl_name: "orch_retry_byte_identical_to_hand_authored_holds"),
  ],
}
